    "output": "This Blueprint Document has been prepared for Archlin to define the supply management solution approach for an enterprise planning implementation in the context of Archlin\u2019s specialty chemicals manufacturing business. Archlin operates with SAP S/4HANA as its ERP system and has an integrated planning environment with some automation already in place. The Phase 1 scope is focused on Supply Management, with particular attention to addressing Archlin\u2019s current pain point of poor inventory planning.\n\nThe solution context for Archlin is centered on improving the planning processes that drive supply decisions, inventory positioning, replenishment planning, and supply plan visibility. The referenced functional requirements describe capabilities for supply planning, inventory planning, demand-driven replenishment, scenario modelling, reporting, approvals, alerts, and integration of cost, pricing, and operational data. These capabilities provide the basis for the Phase 1 design direction, where Archlin\u2019s supply management processes will be supported through configurable planning logic, supply plan reporting, inventory requirement calculations, replenishment rules, and exception visibility.\n\nArchlin\u2019s current planning maturity is characterized as integrated planning with some automation, which indicates that the implementation should build on existing planning foundations while improving consistency, transparency, and responsiveness across the supply management process. The Blueprint therefore establishes a structured solution framework for how Archlin will manage supply plans, calculate inventory requirements, support replenishment decisions, review constraints, and generate reporting to measure plan performance. Where planning inputs such as cost, pricing, lead time, source of supply, stock position, and supply plan data are required, the solution approach relies on integration and maintained planning parameters as described in the source requirements.\n\nThe intended solution will support Archlin\u2019s planners by enabling graphical representation of supply plans and related constraints, configurable reports and analytics for supply planning, supply plan versus actuals reporting based on archived supply plans, and unit-of-measure conversion where conversion factors are maintained. For inventory and replenishment planning, the design references the ability to apply safety stock and cycle stock requirements, calculate safety stock based on future days of demand, respect lot size parameters such as minimum, maximum, and multiple quantities, manage replenishment on and off dates through planning calendars, and support multiple sources of supply using defined prioritization. The solution also supports time-phased management of source of supply and lead time through effective dates, and allows exceptional manual changes to sources where required.\n\nArchlin\u2019s supply management implementation will also consider process governance through approvals, alerts, and workflow-related capabilities. The source requirements describe the ability to notify approvers through alerts based on defined frequency, track supply plan approvals, allow approval or rejection with reason codes and comments, and notify submitters when a supply plan is rejected or approved. These capabilities provide a foundation for controlled supply plan review and decision-making within Archlin\u2019s planning process, subject to final configuration decisions and detailed design confirmation.\n\nScenario planning is an important design consideration for Archlin because the requirements describe the ability to model supply disruptions by altering sourcing networks and understanding cost implications, perform what-if analysis, compare scenarios, and commit scenarios into the baseline plan and budget. For Archlin, this supports a structured approach to evaluating changes in supply assumptions, replenishment plans, sourcing priorities, and financial impacts before committing planning decisions. The solution also supports volume and finance-based projections across the planning horizon, with working capital calculation possible when the necessary inputs are provided.\n\nThe Blueprint also recognizes that several advanced or extended capabilities are identified but are not necessarily part of the initial implementation. For example, inventory optimization is described as not considered in the initial implementation, and expiry-based replenishment exclusion is noted as not considered in the initial implementation, with reporting feasible if considered in a later release. Demand sensing and AI capabilities, advanced forecasting algorithms using external causal factors, and certain advanced forecast-related capabilities are similarly described as not in the scope of the initial release in the source material. For Archlin, these distinctions are important to preserve the Phase 1 focus on Supply Management and to avoid extending the initial design beyond the confirmed implementation scope.\n\nThe expected outcome for Archlin is a more structured and transparent supply management capability that improves visibility into supply plans, replenishment requirements, inventory parameters, planning exceptions, constraints, and approval status. The implementation is intended to help Archlin move from poor inventory planning toward a more controlled planning process supported by configurable rules, maintained master data, integrated planning inputs, scenario analysis, and reporting. Any Archlin-specific process variants, organization details, site counts, role assignments, implementation dates, and detailed integration mappings that are not available in the Archlin source documents remain .",
